1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,000
I hear that there's a Hawaiian custom of something called a hukilau.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Hukilau, hukilau is they have a rope, end of the rope they have a net there.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
They surround that certain place and they drag the net.

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,000
They surround it, you mean they carry the net out?

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Carry the net, uh-huh.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,000
How do they, is there something to keep the net floating?

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Yeah, some have those floaters floating.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Some they lay down on the bottom and they drag that net in.

9
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,000
What do the floats, what are the floats made of?

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,000
The float made of those, some coconut, dry coconut I guess.

11
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Oh, dry coconut.

12
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Yeah.

13
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:46,000
That's a rooster coconut.

14
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,000
And that floats the top of the net?

15
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Top of the net.

16
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Now, the word lau means leaf, doesn't it?

17
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Lau, lau is a leaf.

18
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Yeah, lau is a leaf.

19
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,000
All right, lau, the word is a leaf coming in this word.

20
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Hukilau, the leaves, you know, they sit on the top, top of the rope, so that when they pull, the fish are going to not jump over the rope.

21
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:14,000
The ropes, the leaves keep the fish from jumping over?

22
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:15,000
They scare them away.

23
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,000
They scare them away because they want to go under the water.

24
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:23,000
The moment you go under the water, they will go to the net. They have a pocket on the net.

25
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:25,000
They have a pocket on the net.

26
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Now the bottom of the net, that is...

27
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,000
The bottom of the net, they have a pocket.

28
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,000
The bottom must have some kind of things to make it sink.

29
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,000
Sink is a lid. They have a lid.

30
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Oh, they have a lid on it, I see.

31
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Then somebody goes out and puts the net out around.

32
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:43,000
Yeah, they throw it with a boat.

33
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:44,000
With a boat.

34
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Two boats, they throw it all around.

35
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:50,000
And then who pulls it in?

36
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Well, they have a lot of... on the shows, about 40, 50 guys, they pull that rope up.

37
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Ah, I see.

38
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
But they must have a pretty big school of fish there.

39
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,000
Yeah, once in a while they have a nice school, right?

40
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:13,000
And then they pull them all in and the fish don't, are scared to jump out, so they get caught.

41
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
No, they are all in a net already. They get caught in a net.

42
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:24,000
I suppose the top of the net comes in sooner than the bottom, so if they do try to go out, they're not pocketed.

43
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Yeah, they have to take on, you know, about six or seven guys swimming around the net to watch how the fish are going.

44
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:32,000
Oh, I see.

45
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,000
And then chasing at the hole.

46
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:35,000
Uh-huh.

47
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:41,000
Well, about how much fish do they get out of a good haul of those?

48
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Once I saw up Waimea, they caught about a ton, I guess, of those akule.

49
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,000
Oh, they have a nice crew up there.

50
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:52,000
What's akule?

51
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Um, the akule, some kind of, you know, the Hawaiian fish called akule, akule.

